  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: SQL_AND_OR
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: SQL_AND_OR
  language: 3
  conceptTitle: 'AND and OR: Combining Conditions'
  symbol: AND
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - SQL_04_AND_OR
  - HZ_SQL_03_AND_OR
  topics:
  - AND / OR
  definition: AND and OR join two conditions inside a WHERE clause.
  howItWorks: With AND, a row is kept only when both conditions are true. With OR,
    a row is kept when at least one condition is true. So AND usually returns fewer
    rows, and OR returns more.
  exampleCode: "Table: pets\n id | name  | animal | age\n  1 | Bolt  | Dog    |  
    4\n  2 | Misty | Cat    |   2\n  3 | Rex   | Dog    |   7\n\nSELECT name FROM
    pets\nWHERE animal = 'Dog' AND age < 5;"
  exampleExplanation: 'Bolt is a dog and 4 is less than 5, so both conditions are
    true: Bolt is returned. Rex is a dog, but 7 is not less than 5, so he is left
    out. Misty is not a dog. With OR instead, all three would be returned.'
  tip: Check each row against both conditions, one row at a time.
